@@ -0,0 +1,114 @@
+# Independent OOD generator 0.1
+
+The entry point is `agent_loop_lab.synth_ood.FAMILIES`. Every entry accepts
+`(rng: random.Random, session_id: str, cfg: OracleConfig)` and returns
+`(list[CanonicalEvent], OracleRecord)`. No standard generator implementation,
+datasets, classifiers, weights, network services, or material outside this
+commission directory was consulted.
+
+## World and causal model
+
+`engine.py` implements an in-memory repository with actual text contents. A
+small contract language interprets `enabled=1` as a satisfied shard predicate;
+`enabled=0` is a fault. Each shard also has a `mode` convention. Comments are
+behaviourally inert. Test counts are computed from these predicates, and
+progress is the fraction of satisfied contracts. This is a simulated language
+interpreter, not execution of Elixir, Zig, Lua, or Clojure. Those extensions,
+paths and suite names supply varied tool vocabulary.
+
+Each read of an unexplored contract reveals two material facts: its predicate
+and fixture. The engine maintains a knowledge set, content read history,
+behavioural validation history, control history, previous workspace digests,
+one active episode, and independent mirror/index readiness counters. Different
+request bodies distinguish service retries; backoff metadata does not.
+External success reveals lease knowledge but does not repair repository faults.
+
+Effects come from these state transitions, never from fingerprints or encoded
+features. The initial failure opens no episode. An exact consecutive retry
+opens one; retries/polls consume the supplied config's budgets. Success closes
+the episode and takes precedence over new knowledge. A relevant behavioural
+change closes an abandoned episode immediately; two unrelated operations also
+close it. Progress belongs to the write, never to its subsequent validation.
+Comment churn is redundant despite changing text digests. A return to a prior
+content state records an undo; the shared labeller recomputes reversions for
+each window. Progress, facts and undo flags remain recorded even when a higher
+precedence effect applies.
+
+Canonical digests and fingerprints use only the shared fingerprint module.
+Public events contain operation metadata and observations, with no scenario
+tags or hidden effects. Session identifiers are caller supplied: callers should
+use opaque identifiers when assembling evaluation sets. The sealed record uses
+`generator_version="ood-0.1"`, `scenario_family="ood:<name>"`, and exactly the
+shared `latent_trace` and `label_session` functions. Since the API supplies an
+RNG rather than its original seed, `OracleRecord.seed` is a stream nonce drawn
+from that RNG. Replay requires the caller's original seed or saved RNG state;
+the nonce is not advertised as a replay seed.
+
+## Families
+
+Intended mixes below are minimum scored-label coverage in each session under
+the frozen v0.2 parameters. Ambiguous transition windows are retained. The
+instrumentation family additionally has unscored instrumentation labels.
+
+| Family | Intended scored labels | Mechanics and difference from §7.1 |
+|---|---|---|
+| `shard_migration` | PRODUCTIVE | Reconcile independently broken shard contracts in random order. Each write repairs a predicate and each replica test derives new counts from text. Reimagines incremental repair and improving repeated validation (§7.1.1, 9) as distributed contract migration. |
+| `fixture_archaeology` | PRODUCTIVE | Investigate separate shard predicates and fixture identities, interspersed with distinct planning operations, before making edits. Reimagines exploration (§7.1.2) as material fixture discovery rather than repeated diagnostics. |
+| `lease_reconnect` | RECOVERY, PRODUCTIVE | Several distinct mirror hosts reset lease POST requests two or three times before accepting them; repair surrounds the external episodes. Reimagines transient failures and bounded retries (§7.1.3, 4) through multi-host lease acquisition. |
+| `index_watermark` | RECOVERY, PRODUCTIVE | Distinct replicated indexes return neutral not-ready results, then a lease becomes visible. Backoff changes on every poll but remains outside intent identity. Reimagines legitimate repeated polling (§7.1.10) as sequential quorum-watermark waits. |
+| `pinned_validation` | RECOVERY, STALLED | Select a replica check, then repeatedly invoke the same deterministic failing suite without changing behaviour. The prelude lets early in-policy retries fall inside full windows. Reimagines repeated failed tests (§7.1.5) as a pinned replica assertion that survives far beyond the retry budget. |
+| `annotation_spiral` | STALLED | Append fresh explanatory comments with changing ticket numbers, validating between writes. Every digest is fresh but behaviour and pass counts are fixed. Reimagines ineffective edits and expensive repetition (§7.1.6, 11) as documentation-only repair attempts. |
+| `serializer_duel` | OSCILLATING | Two emitters alternately restore left/right wire conventions in one contract. Neither satisfies any fault predicate. Reimagines workspace/tool oscillation (§7.1.7, 8) as opposing serializers rather than alternative fixes. |
+| `merge_ping_pong` | OSCILLATING | Alternate upstream/local ownership resolutions in a merge configuration, with reconciliation plans between writes. **Additional family absent from the §7.1 list:** merge ownership negotiation rather than a repair task. The simulated conflicting choices are concrete file contents; no real git merge is run. |
+| `lockfile_churn` | OSCILLATING | Installer and resolver alternately pin dependency versions 1 and 2, inspecting the result after each write. **Additional family absent from the §7.1 list:** dependency pin negotiation. Dependency changes do not satisfy shard predicates; no package manager is invoked. |
+| `telemetry_gaps` | PRODUCTIVE | Continue contract repair while independently losing one failure fingerprint, one workspace observation, and one token observation. Reimagines under-instrumentation (§7.1.12) as isolated transport gaps with fully intact latent state and recovery of gate coverage. |
+
+## Distribution choices and instrumentation
+
+The repository has 12–18 contracts, with four language/suite vocabularies,
+varying error classes and generated mirror/resource identities. Family session
+lengths range from 21 to 89 events, with longer plateau and oscillation tails
+than the standard spec's 12–60-event envelope. Ordering, counts, costs,
+timestamps, vocabulary, and service readiness all use the passed RNG. There
+are no global RNG calls, wall-clock reads or I/O during generation.
+
+Costs are zero with probability 0.13; otherwise they are lognormal with
+parameters `(7.5, 1.65)`, capped at 400,000 tokens and divided randomly between
+input/output. This distribution is independent of labels and can exceed the
+encoder's saturation threshold. High spend is not a stall rule.
+
+Missing validation baselines can contaminate later windows. To satisfy the
+commission's exact window-local rejection requirement, telemetry gaps drop
+tokens, workspace digests and failure fingerprints, while retaining suite/count
+observations and complete history. Thus a window fails the gate exactly while
+it contains a dropped event, without causing later baseline rejection.
+
+## Verification
+
+Run from this directory:
+
+```sh
+PYTHONPATH=src .venv/bin/python -m pytest tests_ood -q
+```
+
+The tests cover all ten families over 24 seeds, byte-identical event and sealed
+record serialization, schema round trips, sequence validation, latent
+validation, label recomputation, effect/dropped-index consistency, intended
+label coverage per session, and every full window's gate/encoder path.
+Focused causal checks exercise content-derived progress and test results,
+knowledge, redundant edits, retry/poll limits, resolution precedence,
+abandonment, reversions and all three intentional missing-observation reasons.
+The repair and annotation families also have identical category/outcome
+prefixes with opposite productive/stalled labels, checked explicitly.
+Labels are never selected by examining encoded feature values.
+
+`synth_ood.counterfactuals.counterfactual_pairs(rng, session_id, cfg)` additionally
+provides the three §7.2 paired experiments. Nine byte-identical prefix events
+precede the intervention. A persistent outage replaces a transient one, or an
+edit strategy chooses actual repair versus inert annotations or mode reversal.
+The retry policy resumes useful work after success and continues retrying after
+failure. Endpoints contrast PRODUCTIVE with STALLED or OSCILLATING. Both
+alternatives share their prefix IDs: keep a pair together when splitting data,
+and treat its branches as alternative sessions rather than concatenating them.
+All continuation costs still consume the passed RNG. These pairs are separate
+from `FAMILIES`, whose keys remain the ten scenario families above.
diff --git a/src/agent_loop_lab/synth_ood/__init__.py b/src/agent_loop_lab/synth_ood/__init__.py
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..5d2cf497c900d72d5125d1c95db8da89a1b97b4a
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/agent_loop_lab/synth_ood/__init__.py
@@ -0,0 +1,157 @@
+"""Independent OOD session families. Only shared contracts/oracle are reused.
+
+Each callable accepts (random.Random, session_id, OracleConfig). Intended
+scored labels are stated in its docstring; ambiguous transition windows are
+preserved. All session choices and costs consume only the supplied RNG.
+"""
+
+from collections.abc import Callable
+import random
+
+from ..oracle_config import OracleConfig
+from ..schema import CanonicalEvent, OracleRecord
+from .engine import Engine
+
+
+def _repair(e: Engine, count: int) -> None:
+    paths = e.rng.sample(e.repo.paths, count)
+    for path in paths:
+        e.read(path)
+        e.write(path, e.repo.files[path].replace("enabled=0", "enabled=1"))
+        e.test()
+
+
+def shard_migration(rng, session_id, cfg):
+    """PRODUCTIVE: repair separate shard contracts, with validation between writes."""
+    e = Engine(rng, session_id, cfg)
+    e.test()
+    for path in rng.sample(e.repo.paths, rng.randint(10, len(e.repo.paths))):
+        e.write(path, e.repo.files[path].replace("enabled=0", "enabled=1"))
+        e.test()
+    return e.finish("shard_migration")
+
+
+def fixture_archaeology(rng, session_id, cfg):
+    """PRODUCTIVE: discover paired predicate/fixture facts before repairing anything."""
+    e = Engine(rng, session_id, cfg)
+    for path in rng.sample(e.repo.paths, len(e.repo.paths)):
+        e.read(path)
+        # Interleave a distinct material source with a first-time control step.
+        e.perform("plan", args={"action_type": f"inspect:{path}"})
+    _repair(e, rng.randint(3, 7))
+    return e.finish("fixture_archaeology")
+
+
+def lease_reconnect(rng, session_id, cfg):
+    """RECOVERY and PRODUCTIVE: renewing mirror leases after peer resets."""
+    e = Engine(rng, session_id, cfg)
+    _repair(e, 3)
+    for i in range(rng.randint(3, 6)):
+        host = f"mirror-{i}.cedar.invalid"
+        e.service(host, "retry", rng.randint(2, 3))
+        target = {"method": "POST", "host": host, "path_template": "/lease/{key}"}
+        for _ in range(e.services[host][1] + 1):
+            e.perform("http_call", target, {"body": '{"lease":"replica"}'})
+    _repair(e, 3)
+    return e.finish("lease_reconnect")
+
+
+def index_watermark(rng, session_id, cfg):
+    """RECOVERY and PRODUCTIVE: wait for replicated indexes using bounded polls."""
+    e = Engine(rng, session_id, cfg)
+    _repair(e, 3)
+    for i in range(rng.randint(3, 5)):
+        resource = f"index:quorum:{i}"
+        e.service(resource, "poll", rng.randint(3, 5))
+        for attempt in range(e.services[resource][1] + 1):
+            e.perform("poll_status", resource, {"backoff": 2**attempt})
+    _repair(e, 3)
+    return e.finish("index_watermark")
+
+
+def pinned_validation(rng, session_id, cfg):
+    """RECOVERY and STALLED: endlessly rerun a deterministic replica assertion."""
+    e = Engine(rng, session_id, cfg)
+    for i in range(rng.randint(5, 7)):
+        e.perform("plan", args={"action_type": f"select-replica-check:{i}"})
+    for _ in range(rng.randint(25, 82)):
+        e.test()
+    return e.finish("pinned_validation")
+
+
+def annotation_spiral(rng, session_id, cfg):
+    """STALLED: unique commentary writes preserve the broken executable contract."""
+    e = Engine(rng, session_id, cfg)
+    path = rng.choice(e.repo.paths)
+    base = e.repo.files[path]
+    e.test()
+    for i in range(rng.randint(12, 39)):
+        e.write(path, base + f"# explanation {i}: ticket {rng.randrange(1000000)}\n")
+        e.test()
+    return e.finish("annotation_spiral")
+
+
+def serializer_duel(rng, session_id, cfg):
+    """OSCILLATING: two emitters repeatedly restore opposing wire conventions."""
+    e = Engine(rng, session_id, cfg)
+    path = rng.choice(e.repo.paths)
+    left = e.repo.files[path]
+    right = left.replace("mode=left", "mode=right")
+    for i in range(rng.randint(24, 80)):
+        e.write(path, right if i % 2 == 0 else left)
+    return e.finish("serializer_duel")
+
+
+def merge_ping_pong(rng, session_id, cfg):
+    """OSCILLATING: conflict resolution alternates local and upstream ownership.
+
+    Absent from §7.1: a merge workflow, rather than alternative bug fixes.
+    """
+    e = Engine(rng, session_id, cfg)
+    path = "config/merge.conf"
+    for i in range(rng.randint(12, 29)):
+        e.write(path, "owner=upstream\n" if i % 2 == 0 else "owner=local\n")
+        e.perform("plan", args={"action_type": "reconcile-ownership"})
+    return e.finish("merge_ping_pong")
+
+
+def lockfile_churn(rng, session_id, cfg):
+    """OSCILLATING: installer and resolver pin incompatible dependency versions.
+
+    Absent from §7.1: dependency resolution churn with no repaired contracts.
+    """
+    e = Engine(rng, session_id, cfg)
+    path = "config/replica.lock"
+    for i in range(rng.randint(12, 36)):
+        e.write(path, f"package=cedar\nversion={2 if i % 2 == 0 else 1}\n")
+        e.read(path)
+    return e.finish("lockfile_churn")
+
+
+def telemetry_gaps(rng, session_id, cfg):
+    """PRODUCTIVE plus rejected windows: independent digest/token/failure loss.
+
+    Only non-baseline observations are dropped, so rejection ends exactly when
+    the affected event leaves the window. The underlying repository is intact.
+    """
+    e = Engine(rng, session_id, cfg)
+    _repair(e, 3)
+    e.test(dropped="failure")
+    _repair(e, 3)
+    e.perform("read_file", e.repo.paths[0], dropped="workspace")
+    _repair(e, 3)
+    e.perform("plan", args={"action_type": "audit-transport"}, dropped="tokens")
+    _repair(e, 3)
+    for i in range(rng.randint(0, 8)):
+        e.perform("plan", args={"action_type": f"handoff:{i}"})
+    return e.finish("telemetry_gaps")
+
+
+FAMILIES: dict[str, Callable[[random.Random, str, OracleConfig],
+                           tuple[list[CanonicalEvent], OracleRecord]]] = {
+    f.__name__: f for f in (
+        shard_migration, fixture_archaeology, lease_reconnect, index_watermark,
+        pinned_validation, annotation_spiral, serializer_duel, merge_ping_pong,
+        lockfile_churn, telemetry_gaps,
+    )
+}
diff --git a/src/agent_loop_lab/synth_ood/counterfactuals.py b/src/agent_loop_lab/synth_ood/counterfactuals.py
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..5f8b458563830e94040d754087bcc143c5f6befd
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/agent_loop_lab/synth_ood/counterfactuals.py
@@ -0,0 +1,72 @@
+"""Causal paired continuations required by spec §7.2.
+
+Alternative histories deliberately share session/event IDs in their identical
+prefix. Keep each pair in one dataset partition and never concatenate its two
+alternative histories into a single event stream.
+"""
+
+from copy import deepcopy
+import random
+
+from ..oracle_config import OracleConfig
+from ..schema import CanonicalEvent, OracleRecord
+from .engine import Engine
+
+Session = tuple[list[CanonicalEvent], OracleRecord]
+
+
+def _branch(e: Engine, rng: random.Random) -> Engine:
+    branch = deepcopy(e)
+    # All new random draws still use the caller's RNG. Only world/history state
+    # is forked; no independent or global random stream is introduced.
+    branch.rng = rng
+    return branch
+
+
+def counterfactual_pairs(
+    rng: random.Random, session_id: str, cfg: OracleConfig,
+) -> dict[str, tuple[Session, Session]]:
+    """Return successful/doomed retry, useful/inert edit, useful/reversal pairs.
+
+    Each pair shares nine byte-identical prefix events. The single intervention
+    is service persistence or the edit strategy. The agent's subsequent policy
+    reacts to success by resuming repair, or to failure by continuing retries.
+    """
+    result = {}
+    e = Engine(rng, f"{session_id}:lease", cfg)
+    for i in range(8):
+        e.perform("plan", args={"action_type": f"prepare:{i}"})
+    host = "relay.cedar.invalid"
+    target = {"method": "GET", "host": host, "path_template": "/lease"}
+    e.service(host, "retry", 2)
+    e.perform("http_call", target)
+    persistent = _branch(e, rng)
+    # The only world intervention: a persistent rather than transient outage.
+    persistent.services[host] = ("retry", 1000)
+    for _ in range(2):
+        e.perform("http_call", target)
+    for path in e.repo.paths[:5]:
+        e.read(path)
+        e.write(path, e.repo.files[path].replace("enabled=0", "enabled=1"))
+        e.test()
+    for _ in range(17):
+        persistent.perform("http_call", target)
+    result["service_persistence"] = (e.finish("transient_lease"), persistent.finish("persistent_lease"))
+
+    for strategy in ("annotation", "reversal"):
+        e = Engine(rng, f"{session_id}:{strategy}", cfg)
+        for i in range(8):
+            e.perform("plan", args={"action_type": f"prepare:{i}"})
+        e.test()
+        alternative = _branch(e, rng)
+        path = alternative.repo.paths[0]
+        original = alternative.repo.files[path]
+        for i, repair_path in enumerate(e.repo.paths[:10]):
+            e.write(repair_path, e.repo.files[repair_path].replace("enabled=0", "enabled=1"))
+            e.test()
+            text = (original + f"# repair note {i}\n" if strategy == "annotation" else
+                    original.replace("mode=left", "mode=right") if i % 2 == 0 else original)
+            alternative.write(path, text)
+            alternative.test()
+        result[f"edit_{strategy}"] = (e.finish("effective_edit"), alternative.finish(f"{strategy}_edit"))
+    return result
diff --git a/src/agent_loop_lab/synth_ood/engine.py b/src/agent_loop_lab/synth_ood/engine.py
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..35d5b1100810d8d349631203918e7225c9bc237b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/agent_loop_lab/synth_ood/engine.py
@@ -0,0 +1,239 @@
+"""Independent, small repository interpreter and causal operation engine.
+
+Files are simulated text, never files in the caller's checkout. The interpreted
+contract is deliberately language independent: enabled=1 repairs a contract;
+mode selects a competing deployment convention without repairing a contract.
+"""
+
+from __future__ import annotations
+
+import random
+from datetime import datetime, timedelta, timezone
+
+from ..fingerprint import (
+    FAMILY_CATEGORY, failure_fingerprint, intent_fingerprint, workspace_digest,
+)
+from ..oracle_config import OracleConfig
+from ..schema import CanonicalEvent, LatentEffect as E, OracleRecord, Outcome, Provenance
+from ..synth.labeller import label_session
+from ..synth.latent import LatentStep, latent_trace
+
+
+class Repository:
+    """Textual contracts, independent facts, behaviour, and test interpretation."""
+
+    def __init__(self, rng: random.Random):
+        language, suffix, suite = rng.choice([
+            ("Elixir", "exs", "exunit:replication"),
+            ("Zig", "zig", "zig:allocator"),
+            ("Lua", "lua", "busted:stream"),
+            ("Clojure", "clj", "kaocha:ledger"),
+        ])
+        self.suite = suite
+        self.error_class = rng.choice(["ReplicaDivergence", "AllocatorInvariant", "StreamOrderViolation"])
+        self.paths = [f"lib/shards/contract_{i}.{suffix}" for i in range(rng.randint(12, 18))]
+        self.files = {p: f"# {language} contract {i}\nenabled=0\nmode=left\n"
+                      for i, p in enumerate(self.paths)}
+        self.files["config/replica.lock"] = "package=cedar\nversion=1\n"
+        self.files["config/merge.conf"] = "owner=local\n"
+
+    def behaviour(self) -> tuple:
+        return tuple((p, tuple(line for line in text.splitlines()
+                              if line and not line.startswith("#")))
+                     for p, text in sorted(self.files.items()))
+
+    def passing(self) -> int:
+        return sum("enabled=1" in self.files[p].splitlines() for p in self.paths)
+
+    def progress(self) -> float:
+        return self.passing() / len(self.paths)
+
+    def digest(self) -> str:
+        return workspace_digest(self.files)
+
+
+class Engine:
+    """Apply operations, derive effects from state, then emit observations.
+
+The only scenario input is the operation to attempt. Effects and labels are
+never supplied by scenario scripts. No encoder is imported here.
+"""
+
+    def __init__(self, rng: random.Random, session_id: str, cfg: OracleConfig):
+        self.rng, self.session_id, self.cfg = rng, session_id, cfg
+        # The API receives an RNG rather than its seed. Record a reproducible
+        # stream nonce, not a claim to know the caller's original seed.
+        self.seed = rng.getrandbits(63)
+        self.repo = Repository(rng)
+        self.p0 = self.repo.progress()
+        self.knowledge: set[str] = set()
+        self.inspected: set[tuple] = set()
+        self.validated: set[tuple] = set()
+        self.controls: set[tuple] = set()
+        self.services: dict[str, tuple[str, int]] = {}
+        self.service_calls: dict[str, int] = {}
+        self.events: list[CanonicalEvent] = []
+        self.steps: list[LatentStep] = []
+        self.states = [self.repo.digest()]
+        self.last_failure: tuple | None = None
+        self.episode_key: tuple | None = None
+        self.episode_kind: str | None = None
+        self.attempts = 0
+        self.unrelated = 0
+        self.clock = datetime(2026, 3, 14, tzinfo=timezone.utc)
+
+    def service(self, name: str, kind: str, failures: int) -> None:
+        self.services[name] = (kind, failures)
+        self.service_calls[name] = 0
+
+    def close_episode(self) -> None:
+        self.episode_key = self.episode_kind = None
+        self.attempts = self.unrelated = 0
+
+    def perform(self, family: str, target=None, args=None, *, content=None,
+                dropped: str | None = None) -> None:
+        args = dict(args or {})
+        before, behaviour, previous_p = self.repo.digest(), self.repo.behaviour(), self.repo.progress()
+        facts: set[str] = set()
+        redundant = False
+        outcome, error_class, produced = Outcome.SUCCESS, None, False
+        error_location = None
+        passed = total = suite = None
+        kind = None
+        # Relevant state for retry equality is behavioural, so cosmetic edits
+        # do not make a deterministic failing validation a fresh attempt.
+        key = (family, repr(target), repr(sorted(args.items())), behaviour)
+        if family == "read_file":
+            text = self.repo.files[target]
+            read_key = (target, text)
+            redundant = read_key in self.inspected
+            self.inspected.add(read_key)
+            if target in self.repo.paths:
+                facts = {f"{target}:predicate", f"{target}:fixture"} - self.knowledge
+                redundant = not facts
+        elif family == "write_file":
+            args = {"content": content}
+            self.repo.files[target] = content
+            redundant = self.repo.behaviour() == behaviour
+        elif family == "run_tests":
+            produced, suite = True, target
+            passed, total = self.repo.passing(), len(self.repo.paths)
+            validation_key = (target, tuple(sorted(args.get("tests", []))), behaviour)
+            key = (family, validation_key)
+            redundant = validation_key in self.validated
+            self.validated.add(validation_key)
+            if passed < total:
+                outcome, error_class, kind = Outcome.ERROR, self.repo.error_class, "retry"
+                error_location = next(p for p in self.repo.paths if "enabled=1" not in self.repo.files[p].splitlines())
+        elif family in ("http_call", "poll_status"):
+            resource = target if family == "poll_status" else target["host"]
+            error_location = resource
+            kind, failures = self.services[resource]
+            self.service_calls[resource] += 1
+            # Caller metadata such as backoff never distinguishes an attempt.
+            key = ((family, resource) if family == "poll_status" else
+                   (family, tuple(sorted(target.items())), args.get("body")))
+            if self.service_calls[resource] <= failures:
+                outcome = Outcome.NEUTRAL if kind == "poll" else Outcome.ERROR
+                error_class = "LeaseNotReady" if kind == "poll" else "PeerReset"
+            else:
+                facts = {f"service:{resource}:lease"} - self.knowledge
+        elif family == "plan":
+            control_key = (family, args["action_type"])
+            redundant = control_key in self.controls
+            self.controls.add(control_key)
+        else:
+            raise ValueError(f"unsupported simulated operation: {family}")
+
+        after = self.repo.digest()
+        mutation = before != after
+        p = self.repo.progress()
+        new_facts = len(facts)
+        self.knowledge.update(facts)
+        undo = mutation and after in self.states and len(self.states) > 1
+        recent_states = {s.ws_before for s in self.steps[-7:]} | {s.ws_after for s in self.steps[-7:]}
+        recent_states.add(before)
+        reversion = undo and after in recent_states
+
+        failed = kind is not None and outcome in (Outcome.ERROR, Outcome.NEUTRAL)
+        exact_retry = key == self.last_failure
+        resolved = False
+        policy_effect = None
+        if exact_retry:
+            if self.episode_key != key:
+                self.close_episode()
+                self.episode_key, self.episode_kind = key, kind
+            self.attempts += 1
+            self.unrelated = 0
+            if not failed:
+                resolved = True
+                self.close_episode()
+            else:
+                limit = self.cfg.p_max if kind == "poll" else self.cfg.r_max
+                policy_effect = (E.IN_POLICY_POLL if kind == "poll" else E.IN_POLICY_RETRY) if self.attempts <= limit else E.REDUNDANT
+        else:
+            if self.episode_kind:
+                self.unrelated += 1
+                if self.repo.behaviour() != behaviour or self.unrelated >= 2:
+                    self.close_episode()
+        self.last_failure = key if failed else None
+
+        if resolved:
+            effect = E.RESOLVES_EPISODE
+        elif p > previous_p:
+            effect = E.PROGRESS
+        elif new_facts:
+            effect = E.NEW_FACT
+        elif reversion:
+            effect = E.REVERSION
+        elif policy_effect is not None:
+            effect = policy_effect
+        elif redundant:
+            effect = E.REDUNDANT
+        else:
+            effect = E.NONE
+
+        i = len(self.events)
+        self.steps.append(LatentStep(
+            i, effect, p, new_facts, before, after, mutation, undo,
+            self.episode_kind, self.attempts, resolved, dropped is not None,
+        ))
+        # A zero-inflated, heavy-tailed cost law, independent of labels.
+        cost = 0 if self.rng.random() < .13 else min(400000, int(self.rng.lognormvariate(7.5, 1.65)))
+        fraction = self.rng.uniform(.35, .96)
+        input_tokens = int(cost * fraction)
+        self.clock += timedelta(milliseconds=self.rng.randint(30, 1700))
+        self.events.append(CanonicalEvent(
+            session_id=self.session_id, agent_id="worker", task_id="contract-maintenance",
+            event_id=f"{self.session_id}:{i}", sequence_index=i,
+            timestamp=self.clock.isoformat(), operation_family=family,
+            operation_category=FAMILY_CATEGORY[family], outcome=outcome,
+            intent_fingerprint=intent_fingerprint(family, target, args),
+            failure_fingerprint=(None if dropped == "failure" else failure_fingerprint(family, error_class, error_location)) if outcome is Outcome.ERROR else None,
+            workspace_state_before=None if dropped == "workspace" else before,
+            workspace_state_after=None if dropped == "workspace" else after,
+            validation_produced=produced, validation_suite_id=suite,
+            validation_passed=passed, validation_total=total,
+            input_tokens=None if dropped == "tokens" else input_tokens,
+            output_tokens=cost-input_tokens,
+            provenance=Provenance(source="simulator:ood-0.1"),
+        ))
+        self.states.append(after)
+
+    def read(self, path: str) -> None:
+        self.perform("read_file", path)
+
+    def write(self, path: str, text: str) -> None:
+        self.perform("write_file", path, content=text)
+
+    def test(self, *, dropped=None) -> None:
+        self.perform("run_tests", self.repo.suite, {"tests": ["replica-contract"]}, dropped=dropped)
+
+    def finish(self, name: str) -> tuple[list[CanonicalEvent], OracleRecord]:
+        return self.events, OracleRecord(
+            session_id=self.session_id, generator_version="ood-0.1", seed=self.seed,
+            scenario_family=f"ood:{name}", event_effects=tuple(s.effect for s in self.steps),
+            window_labels=label_session(self.steps, self.p0, self.cfg),
+            dropped_observations=tuple(s.sequence_index for s in self.steps if s.dropped),
+            latent_trace=latent_trace(self.steps, self.p0),
+        )
